{"track":"precalc","num":"5.02","slug":"the-dot-product","title":"The Dot Product","sections":[{"heading":"Section 1: Algebraic Definition and Properties","questions":[{"q":"Find the dot product $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}$ for $\\mathbf{u} = \\langle 4, -3 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle 2, 5 \\rangle$.","choices":["$-7$","$23$","$\\langle 8, -15 \\rangle$","$1$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = u_1 v_1 + u_2 v_2 = (4)(2) + (-3)(5) = 8 - 15 = -7$.","figure":{"type":"triangle","vertices":[[0,0],[6,0],[6,4.5]],"labels":["A","C","B"],"side_labels":["6","4.5","7.5"],"right_angle_at":1,"fill":true,"width":145}},{"q":"What is the value of $\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$?","choices":["$\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|^2$","$\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|$","$0$","$1$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = v_1^2 + v_2^2 = \\|\\mathbf{v}\\|^2$."},{"q":"Which property does NOT hold for the dot product?","choices":["$\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ (Commutative)","$c(\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}) = (c\\mathbf{u}) \\cdot \\mathbf{v}$","$\\mathbf{u} \\cdot (\\mathbf{v} + \\mathbf{w}) = \\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} + \\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{w}$","$\\mathbf{u} \\cdot (\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{w}) = (\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}) \\cdot \\mathbf{w}$ (Associative)"],"correct":3,"answer":"The dot product of two vectors is a scalar, so $\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{w}$ is a scalar. You cannot dot a vector with a scalar, so associativity of dot products is undefined."}]},{"heading":"Section 2: Geometric Definition and Angle Between Vectors","questions":[{"q":"State the formula for the cosine of the angle $\\theta$ between two non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$.","choices":["$\\cos\\theta = \\frac{\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}}{\\|\\mathbf{u}\\| \\|\\mathbf{v}\\|}$","$\\cos\\theta = \\frac{\\|\\mathbf{u}\\| \\|\\mathbf{v}\\|}{\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}}$","$\\cos\\theta = \\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}$","$\\cos\\theta = \\frac{\\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{v}}{\\|\\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{v}\\|}$"],"correct":0,"answer":"From $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = \\|\\mathbf{u}\\| \\|\\mathbf{v}\\|\\cos\\theta$, we have $\\cos\\theta = \\frac{\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}}{\\|\\mathbf{u}\\| \\|\\mathbf{v}\\|}$."},{"q":"Find the angle between $\\mathbf{u} = \\langle 1, 1 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle 0, 2 \\rangle$.","choices":["$45^\\circ$","$90^\\circ$","$30^\\circ$","$60^\\circ$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = 0 + 2 = 2$. $\\|\\mathbf{u}\\| = \\sqrt{2}, \\|\\mathbf{v}\\| = 2$. $\\cos\\theta = \\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\Rightarrow \\theta = 45^\\circ$.","figure":{"type":"triangle","vertices":[[0,0],[12,0],[12,5]],"labels":["A","C","B"],"side_labels":["12","5","13"],"right_angle_at":1,"fill":true,"width":145}},{"q":"If the dot product $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} < 0$, what type of angle is $\\theta$?","choices":["Obtuse ($90^\\circ < \\theta \\leq 180^\\circ$)","Acute ($0^\\circ \\leq \\theta < 90^\\circ$)","Right ($90^\\circ$)","Straight ($180^\\circ$)"],"correct":0,"answer":"Since $\\cos\\theta = \\frac{\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}}{\\|\\mathbf{u}\\| \\|\\mathbf{v}\\|}$, a negative dot product implies $\\cos\\theta < 0$, which corresponds to an obtuse angle."}]},{"heading":"Section 3: Orthogonality and Parallel Vectors","questions":[{"q":"Two non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are orthogonal (perpendicular) if and only if:","choices":["$\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = 0$","$\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = 1$","$\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$","$\\|\\mathbf{u}\\| = \\|\\mathbf{v}\\|$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = \\|\\mathbf{u}\\|\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|\\cos(90^\\circ) = 0$."},{"q":"Find $k$ such that $\\mathbf{u} = \\langle 3, k \\rangle$ is orthogonal to $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle 4, 6 \\rangle$.","choices":["$-2$","$2$","$-1/2$","$12$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = 3(4) + 6k = 12 + 6k = 0 \\Rightarrow 6k = -12 \\Rightarrow k = -2$."},{"q":"Which pair of vectors is parallel?","choices":["$\\langle 2, -3 \\rangle$ and $\\langle -6, 9 \\rangle$","$\\langle 2, 3 \\rangle$ and $\\langle 3, 2 \\rangle$","$\\langle 1, 0 \\rangle$ and $\\langle 0, 1 \\rangle$","$\\langle 4, 1 \\rangle$ and $\\langle -1, 4 \\rangle$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$\\langle -6, 9 \\rangle = -3\\langle 2, -3 \\rangle$. A vector is parallel to another if it is a scalar multiple of it.","figure":{"type":"plot","fns":["-(x - 2)**2 + 5","x - 1"],"xmin":-1,"xmax":5,"ymin":-2,"ymax":6,"points":[[2,5],[0,1],[3,4]],"width":145}}]},{"heading":"Section 4: Vector Projections and Work","questions":[{"q":"State the formula for the vector projection of $\\mathbf{u}$ onto $\\mathbf{v}$ ($\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{v}}\\mathbf{u}$).","choices":["$\\left(\\frac{\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}}{\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|^2}\\right)\\mathbf{v}$","$\\left(\\frac{\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}}{\\|\\mathbf{u}\\|^2}\\right)\\mathbf{u}$","$\\frac{\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}}{\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|}$","$(\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v})\\mathbf{v}$"],"correct":0,"answer":"The vector projection is the scalar component $\\frac{\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}}{\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|}$ multiplied by the unit vector $\\frac{\\mathbf{v}}{\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|}$, yielding $\\left(\\frac{\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}}{\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|^2}\\right)\\mathbf{v}$.","figure":{"type":"plot","fns":["1.5*x - 1","-0.75*x + 3.5"],"xmin":-2,"xmax":5,"ymin":-3,"ymax":6,"points":[[2,2],[0,-1]],"width":145}},{"q":"Find $\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{v}}\\mathbf{u}$ for $\\mathbf{u} = \\langle 6, 2 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle 4, 0 \\rangle$.","choices":["$\\langle 6, 0 \\rangle$","$\\langle 4, 0 \\rangle$","$\\langle 0, 2 \\rangle$","$\\langle 24, 0 \\rangle$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = 24$. $\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|^2 = 16$. $\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{v}}\\mathbf{u} = \\frac{24}{16}\\langle 4, 0 \\rangle = \\frac{3}{2}\\langle 4, 0 \\rangle = \\langle 6, 0 \\rangle$."},{"q":"A force $\\mathbf{F} = \\langle 30, 40 \\rangle\\text{ N}$ moves a cart along the displacement $\\mathbf{d} = \\langle 5, 0 \\rangle\\text{ m}$. Calculate the work done.","choices":["$150\\text{ J}$","$200\\text{ J}$","$350\\text{ J}$","$250\\text{ J}$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$W = \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{d} = (30)(5) + (40)(0) = 150\\text{ J}$."}]}]}